What jitterDatAT Is

Docket item 1 (SEQ 387), the datA misread, R0 reproducer. Born red 2026-10-20 on trunk.

Each action is compiled by the jitter at its first call. Every condition is a field accessor compared against 0,
written with its arm on its own indented line (the one-line form `if c; s;` is handed to run time whole and never
compiles the compare, so it agrees by not being compiled). kres holds no data until the line `kres = 5;`.

The top-level `zz = 5 - 2;` and `zz = 3 - 3;` run interpreted and leave tempField holding 3 and then 0. The compiled
compare reads tempField as it stood AT BUILD (runAccCompare's copy-out, GroupActions.rtn:1065, hands the compare a
fresh acRun copy with no jit value, and jitSeedOperands bakes a load of that copy's count), so:

  DA-1  kres.datA != 0, compiled with tempField 3: intended no ARM (the twin), today ARM.
  DA-2  the generator's bare spelling (IncantForms/WorkingOn/parser:18), datA != 0 on argument kres, the same
        history: intended no ARM, today ARM. This is generateParse's data exit taken for a data-less rule.
  DA-C  kop.datA != 0, kop has data: ARM on both roads. The non-zero control; it cannot tell the two reads apart and
        is not meant to; it proves the arm can be reached.
  DA-F  kres.datA + 0 != 0, the same history: the dot sits in a FOLD, so its copy-out is runAccFold's
        (GroupActions.rtn:1091), not the compare's. Intended no ARM, today ARM.
  DA-Y  yy = kres.datA + 1: intended 1 (the twin), today 0 whatever the history -- the jitted store writes the DOT's
        product into yy, not the sum (gJitDotProduct answering a later step). Pinned, not cured (ruled, 2026-10-20).
  DA-3  kres.datA != 0, compiled with tempField 0, called twice: before `kres = 5;` no ARM on both roads (right by
        luck of the history), after it intended ARM, today none. The read is frozen at build.

The interpreted twin is this file with the `jitter;` line deleted (genLadder/pop.sh does it).
